OSP Web Event Service
Open Service Platform

!!! Introduction

The Poco::OSP::WebEvent::WebEventService is a service that allows a web application
server to notify clients running in a browser, using a RFC 6455
WebSocket and a subject-based publish-subscribe mechanism.

The way this service works is explained in the following section.


!!! The WebEvent Protocol

JavaScript code running in the browser will open a WebSocket connection
to the OSP web server (using path "/webevent") and send a single-frame 
text subscription message specifying the subjects to subscribe to. The format 
of the subscription message is:

    SUBSCRIBE <subjectList> WebEvent/1.0
----

where <subjectList> is a comma-separated list of subject names not
containing white space characters. A comma inside brackets ([], () or {})
belongs to the subject name. An empty subject name makes the message invalid.

Subject names use a hierarchical, reverse DNS-style syntax, with the
parts of the subject delimited by periods. Subject names must not
contain spaces and their parts should be valid C++ identifiers. 
However, the latter restriction is not enforced. 
Subject names are hierarchical. A subscriber to
a "parent" subject (e.g., "com.appinf.events") will also receive
all "child" subjects (e.g., "com.appinf.events.someEvent" and 
"com.appinf.events.someOtherEvent").

A client can unsubscribe from specific events by either sending an
unsubscribe request or by simply closing the WebSocket. The
format of the UNSUBSCRIBE message is similar to the SUBSCRIBE message:

    UNSUBSCRIBE <subjectList>|* WebEvent/1.0
----

where <subjectList> is a comma-separated list of subject names, as
with the SUBSCRIBE message. Instead of a subject list, a single
asterisk can be specified, which means unsubscribe from all
subjects.

After the initial SUBSCRIBE message the client can send additional
SUBSCRIBE or UNSUBSCRIBE messages, and thus dynamically change its 
subscriptions without having to re-establish the WebSocket.

Once a client has opened a WebSocket and sent the SUBSCRIBE
message, the WebSocket connection will be "parked" on the server
and only used when an event message needs to be sent to the
client. This allows a very high number of simultaneous subscribers,
since a single subscriber does not consume much server resources,
except for the socket and some data overhead.
Specifically, a specific thread for the WebSocket connection will only be 
used during the initial connection setup.

Notification messages sent to clients are single-frame text
messages with the following format:

    NOTIFY <subjectName> WebEvent/1.0\r\n
    <data>
----

where <subjectName> is the subjectName specified in the
call to notify(), and <data> is the given data string, which
typically should be a valid serialized JSON or XML document in UTF-8 
encoding, although any valid UTF-8 string can be sent.

If the osp.web.event.clientNotify configuration property is true, a client
can also send notifications to other clients by sending a NOTIFY message
to the server. Otherwise such messages are dropped.

A client can send a NOTIFY message using the special subject
"system.ping" to the server, and the server will respond with a 
NOTIFY using the subject "system.pong", whatever the setting of
osp.web.event.clientNotify. This can be used as a heartbeat
mechanism to verify that the connection to the server is alive.
Any data sent with the ping message will be sent back with the pong
message.


!!! Using the WebEventService

The WebEventService is implemented in the <*com.appinf.osp.webevent*> bundle
and registered under the service name "com.appinf.osp.webevent".


!! On the Server (C++)

To obtain the service:

    Poco::OSP::WebEvent::WebEventService::Ptr pWebEventService = 
        Poco::OSP::ServiceFinder::find<Poco::OSP::WebEvent::WebEventService>(pContext);
----

To send a notification to all subscribers to a given subject, use the
Poco::OSP::WebEvent::WebEventService::notify() method, specifying the
subject name and payload. Note that the payload must be an UTF-8 character
sequence - typically a JSON or XML document.

    pWebEventService->notify("sample.event", "Some event data");
----

To receive notifications on a specific subject, use the 
Poco::OSP::WebEvent::subjectNotified() member function, specifying
the subject name. The function will return a reference to a 
Poco::BasicEvent object which can be used to register a delegate
receiving the notification.

    pWebEventService->subjectNotified("sample.event") += Poco::delegate(onEvent);
----

The delegate function then looks like:

    void onEvent(const Poco::OSP::WebEvent::WebEventService::NotificationEvent& ev)
    {
        const std::string& subject = ev.first;
        const std::string& data = ev.second;
        // ...
    }
----

The Poco::OSP::WebEvent::WebEventService::NotificationEvent type is simply a 
<[std::pair<std::string, std::string>]>, with the first item containing the
subject name and the second item containing the data.

To send a notification only to the connections of one user, use
Poco::OSP::WebEvent::WebEventService::notifyUser(). The user a connection
belongs to is determined when the connection is established, by the resolver
installed with Poco::OSP::WebEvent::WebEventService::setUserResolver(), for
example by looking up the web session referenced by the request's cookies:

    pWebEventService->setUserResolver(
        [](const Poco::Net::HTTPServerRequest& request)
        {
            return usernameFromSession(request); // empty if anonymous
        });
    pWebEventService->notifyUser("alice", "sample.event", "Some event data");
----

Connections for which the resolver returned an empty string, or that were
established without a resolver, belong to no user and are never notified by
notifyUser(). Pass nullptr to setUserResolver() to remove the resolver, for
example when the bundle that installed it stops.

Poco::OSP::WebEvent::WebEventService::listSubscribers() returns the user, page
and client address of every connection. The page is the value of the "page"
query parameter of the upgrade request (for example
"/webevent?page=/app/index.html"), or its Referer header if there is no such
parameter; browsers send no Referer with WebSocket upgrade requests.


!! On the Client (JavaScript)

The following JavaScript module (webevent.js) can be used on the client to subscribe to, and receive
event notifications.

    // webevent.js
    
    var WebEvent = 
    {
        webSocket: null,
    
        available: function()
            {
                return "WebSocket" in window;
            },
        
        connect: function()
            {
                if (!WebEvent.available()) return false;

                var wss = window.location.protocol == "https:" ? "wss://" : "ws://";
                WebEvent.webSocket = new WebSocket(wss + window.location.host + "/webevent");    
            
                WebEvent.webSocket.onopen = function()      
                {
                    if (WebEvent.onConnect) WebEvent.onConnect();
                }; 
            
                WebEvent.webSocket.onmessage = function(evt)      
                {
                    var msg = evt.data;
                    var parsedEvent = WebEvent.parseEvent(msg);
                    if (parsedEvent.method == "NOTIFY")
                    {
                        WebEvent.onNotify(parsedEvent);
                    }
                };
            
                WebEvent.webSocket.onclose = function()
                {
                    if (WebEvent.onDisconnect) WebEvent.onDisconnect();
                    WebEvent.webSocket = null;
                };
            
                WebEvent.webSocket.onerror = function()
                {
                    if (WebEvent.onError) WebEvent.onError();
                };
            
                return true;
            },
        
        disconnect: function()
            {
                if (WebEvent.webSocket) 
                {
                    WebEvent.webSocket.close();
                    WebEvent.webSocket = null;
                }
            
            },

        subscribe: function (subject)
            {
                if (WebEvent.webSocket)
                {
                    var msg = "SUBSCRIBE " + subject + " WebEvent/1.0";
                    WebEvent.webSocket.send(msg);
                }
            },
      
        unsubscribe: function (subject)
            {
                if (WebEvent.webSocket)
                {
                    var msg = "UNSUBSCRIBE " + subject + " WebEvent/1.0";
                    WebEvent.webSocket.send(msg);
                }
            },
        
        notify: function (subject, data)
            {
                if (WebEvent.webSocket)
                {
                    var msg = "NOTIFY " + subject + " WebEvent/1.0\r\n" + data;
                    WebEvent.webSocket.send(msg);
                }
            },

        onConnect: function() {},

        onDisconnect: function() {},
    
        onError: function() {},
    
        onNotify: function(evt) {},
    
        parseEvent: function(msg)
            {
                var event = {};
                var i = 0;
                var methodStart = i;
                while (i < msg.length && msg[i] != ' ' && msg[i] != '\r' && msg[i] != '\n') i++;
                event.method = msg.substring(methodStart, i);
                while (i < msg.length && msg[i] == ' ') i++;
                var subjectStart = i;
                while (i < msg.length && msg[i] != ' ' && msg[i] != '\r' && msg[i] != '\n') i++;
                event.subject = msg.substring(subjectStart, i);
                while (i < msg.length && msg[i] == ' ') i++;
                var versionStart = i;
                while (i < msg.length && msg[i] != ' ' && msg[i] != '\r' && msg[i] != '\n') i++;
                event.version = msg.substring(versionStart, i);
                while (i < msg.length && (msg[i] == '\r' || msg[i] == '\n')) i++;
                event.data = msg.substring(i);      
                return event;
            }
    };
----

To subscribe to an event, embed the following JavaScript in a web page:

    <script type="text/javascript" src="webevent.js"></script>
    <script>
    if (WebEvent.available())
    {
        WebEvent.onNotify = function(evt)
            {
               var subject = evt.subject;
               var data = evt.data;
               // ...
            };
        
        WebEvent.onConnect = function()
            {
                WebEvent.subscribe("sample.event");
            };

        WebEvent.connect();
    }
    </script>
----


!!! Security Considerations

A browser does not restrict a WebSocket connection to the origin of the page
that opens it, and it sends the user's cookies with the upgrade request. Any
page the user visits can therefore try to connect to the WebEventService.
The service checks the following before a connection is established:

  - The Origin header. An upgrade request whose Origin is neither the
    request's own (the host and port of its Host header) nor one of the
    origins listed in osp.web.event.allowedOrigins is rejected with status
    403. The request's own origin has the scheme of the connection (https
    for a secure one), and the host and port of the Host header. Requests
    without an Origin header come from clients other than browsers and are
    accepted.
  - The number of connections, if osp.web.event.maxWebSockets is set.

The service does not authenticate or authorize clients itself. An application
that must restrict access registers a delegate for the requestReceived event,
which is fired for every upgrade request that passed the checks above. The
delegate can inspect the request, for example its session cookie, and reject
the connection by setting the status of the response to anything other than
200 (OK).

The resolver installed with setUserResolver() determines the user a
connection belongs to, and notifyUser() delivers only to the connections of
that user. The resolver runs for every connection that requestReceived has
accepted; it identifies the user but rejects nothing.

The page reported by listSubscribers() is taken from the request's "page"
query parameter or its Referer header. It is supplied by the client, is not
checked, and must be escaped before it is shown in a page.

Clients cannot send notifications to other clients unless
osp.web.event.clientNotify is set to true. When it is set, any connected
client can notify any subject, and the delegates of notificationSent and
subjectNotified cannot tell such a notification from one made by notify().

Event messages reach every client that has subscribed to their subject. The
following guidelines should be observed:

  - Do not include "sensitive" data in an event message sent with notify().
    The definition of "sensitive" is up to the specific implementation, but
    it's best to treat any application data as sensitive. Use notifyUser()
    for data meant for one user only.
  - Use cryptographic hashes or tokens to refer to actual data. For example, when notifying
    a client that a specific data object on the server has been modified, and the client
    needs to update it's representation of it, send a cryptographic hash identifying the object
    in the event message. Only authorized clients already in possession of the hash can then
    associate the event with the object, and the event message is meaningless to everyone else.


!!! Delivery Semantics and Flow Control

Notifications are delivered to each subscriber through a per-subscriber send queue,
drained by a pool of worker threads (see osp.web.event.workers). A worker sends one
frame at a time and waits while the subscriber's connection does not take it. While
fewer subscribers are stalled than there are workers, the other subscribers are served
without delay. When every worker is held by a stalled subscriber, delivery to all
subscribers waits until one of those subscribers is disconnected, which takes at most
about eleven seconds: the send timeout of ten seconds, checked once a second.

Delivery to a subscriber that cannot keep up is lossy:

  - While a frame for a subject is still queued for a subscriber, a newer notification
    for the same subject replaces the queued frame (latest value wins). Frames are
    always delivered in the order the notifications were made.
  - A subscriber with overlapping subscriptions (e.g., to a subject and one of its
    ancestors) receives a single frame per notification.
  - The number of queued frames per subscriber is limited by the
    osp.web.event.maxPendingFrames configuration property. When the limit is
    exceeded, the oldest queued frame for that subscriber is dropped and a warning
    is logged.
  - A subscriber to which a frame has not been sent completely within ten seconds
    is disconnected and removed, within about another second. The limit applies
    to the whole frame, so a client that reads a few bytes at a time cannot hold
    a worker longer.

NOTE: On Windows, a blocking send of a frame to a subscriber that does not read
completes at once: the system takes the whole frame, of any size, into its kernel
buffers. There, neither osp.web.event.maxPendingFrames nor the send timeout limits
the memory that such frames take.

In the other direction, a client that has started a frame and does not send
it completely within ten seconds is disconnected and removed as well, within
about another second.

When the server closes a connection, a worker waits at most two seconds for the
client to confirm the close, within about another second; then the connection is
closed without the confirmation.

Server-side events (notificationSent, subjectNotified) are not affected by any of
this and fire for every notification.


!!! Configuring the WebEventService

The WebEventService provides the following configuration properties in the global
application configuration.


!! osp.web.event.maxWebSockets

Limit the number of WebSocket connections (and thus connected clients).
Specify 0 for an unlimited (as far as system resources allow) number of connections.
Defaults to 0 (unlimited).


!! osp.web.event.maxSubjects

The maximum number of subjects one connection can subscribe to. A SUBSCRIBE
message beyond the limit closes the connection. Specify 0 for no limit.
Defaults to 64.


!! osp.web.event.workers

The number of worker threads used for sending and receiving WebSocket frames,
at most 64. Defaults to two times the number of processor cores.


!! osp.web.event.maxPendingFrames

The maximum number of outbound notification frames queued per subscriber.
When a subscriber cannot keep up and the limit is exceeded, the oldest queued
frame for that subscriber is dropped. Specify 0 for unlimited queueing (which
can exhaust memory if clients stall).
Defaults to 256.


!! osp.web.event.allowedOrigins

A comma-separated list of origins (for example "https://app.example.com") from
which browsers may connect, in addition to the origin of the service itself.
Defaults to an empty list.

Note that behind a proxy that terminates TLS, the service sees plain HTTP
connections, and the public origin of the pages (for example
"https://app.example.com") must be listed here.


!! osp.web.event.clientNotify

Set to true to pass NOTIFY messages sent by clients on to the other
subscribers and to the server's delegates. Defaults to false, which drops such
messages.


!! osp.web.event.pingInterval

The keepalive sweep interval in seconds. Every interval, subscribers that have
not sent anything for at least half the interval are sent a WebSocket PING
control frame (browsers answer with a PONG automatically), and subscribers that
have stayed silent for twice the interval are considered dead and removed.
This reclaims connections whose peer disappeared without closing the socket
(network drop, suspend, power loss). Specify 0 to disable the keepalive sweep.
Defaults to 60.
